  "definition": "The null hypothesis is a statement in statistics that suggests there is no significant difference or effect. It serves as a baseline against which we can test our data. For example, if you're testing whether a new drug works, the null hypothesis might be that the drug has no effect on patients. We often denote this as H0. The p-value is a measure of the evidence against the null hypothesis. A small p-value (typically ≤ 0.05) indicates strong evidence against the null hypothesis, so you reject it. Conversely, a large p-value (> 0.05) indicates weak evidence against the null hypothesis, and you fail to reject it. Essentially, if your p-value is less than 0.05, you have enough evidence to say that your results are statistically significant and not due to chance.",
  "example": "If you're testing a new teaching method and find a p-value of 0.03, this means there's only a 3% chance that the observed improvement in student performance is due to random variation. Therefore, you would reject the null hypothesis that the new teaching method has no effect."
